WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.200
how to plan your strength training week, written by David Robertson, online strength coach.

00:06.000 --> 00:10.400
Already following a strength program, but struggling to fit its sessions into your week,

00:11.120 --> 00:14.400
start by comparing the training you planned with the training you completed.

00:15.200 --> 00:19.440
The biggest planning mistake I see in strength training is committing to a training program

00:19.440 --> 00:24.560
without deciding when its sessions will happen. A training matrix is a simple table showing

00:24.560 --> 00:28.800
which workout belongs on which day. In the article's fictional example,

00:28.800 --> 00:34.240
Alex has four strength sessions each week. He plans an upper body session on Monday,

00:34.240 --> 00:39.920
a lower body session on Tuesday, another upper body session on Thursday, and another lower body

00:39.920 --> 00:46.320
session on Friday. Wednesday and the weekend are days off. He uses this schedule for three weeks.

00:46.320 --> 00:52.560
This is an illustration, session order, frequency, and spacing depend on the trainee in the program.

00:53.120 --> 00:58.880
At the end of three weeks, Alex reviews what happened. In week one, he completed all four

00:58.880 --> 01:04.320
planned strength sessions. In week two, he completed three, missing Tuesday because of a meeting.

01:05.120 --> 01:09.920
In week three, he completed three again, missing Tuesday because of a work deadline.

01:10.880 --> 01:15.440
That adds up to 10 of 12 planned strength sessions or about 83%.

01:16.320 --> 01:20.720
The useful finding is that both missed sessions were Tuesday's lower body workout.

01:21.520 --> 01:25.680
His overall attendance hides a repeated gap in the work tied to his squat goal.

01:26.400 --> 01:31.200
When the same session repeatedly gets missed, review whether it's timing and demands fit your

01:31.200 --> 01:37.280
week. Three weeks gives you a practical starting point for that review. Your weekly matrix sets out

01:37.280 --> 01:43.280
when the sessions happen. Periodization means planning how the training itself changes over time,

01:43.280 --> 01:48.320
including the amount of work and how heavy it is. Does your training week keep breaking down?

01:49.120 --> 01:54.800
In a free 20 minute discovery call, we'll discuss your goals, training history and schedule,

01:54.800 --> 02:04.000
and explore whether ongoing coaching fits your needs.

